{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"Repair the invalid sudo configuration.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The original syntax error was directly observed, a candidate with only the broken line removed parsed successfully, the repaired file was installed with correct ownership, mode, and SELinux context, and both the main file and opsadmin drop-in still parsed successfully after reboot.","evidence_ids":["cmd-8d09d3f3b2ec4d0e","cmd-75be746fefcd43c8","cmd-ee2a2dba91bf4b05","cmd-c2b9f8cdba994b9c"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"Return node1 to normal multi-user operation.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The executor changed the default target from rescue to multi-user, successfully isolated into it, established a healthy pre-reboot baseline, and demonstrated after reboot that the system was running with multi-user and SSH active, rescue inactive, multi-user as the default, and no failed units.","evidence_ids":["prepared-recovery-state","cmd-1d53fd76a39e43c5","cmd-53c59ab0b5f04ab1","cmd-c2b9f8cdba994b9c","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets"]},{"id":"R3","requirement":"Preserve the existing opsadmin account and its non-interactive administrative access.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The prepared identity was uid/gid 1000 with wheel membership. Before repair the existing NOPASSWD drop-in was observed; after repair and again after reboot, commands executed as opsadmin through sudo -n obtained uid 0. This directly demonstrates preservation of the account's required non-interactive administrative behavior.","evidence_ids":["opsadmin-identity","opsadmin-policy-hash","cmd-db1b8d2ddcca4685","cmd-9c8033d387654091","cmd-c2b9f8cdba994b9c"]},{"id":"R4","requirement":"Leave /srv/operations/keep.txt unchanged.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Its final and post-reboot SHA-256 exactly matches the prepared baseline, and its observed ownership, mode, and size remained opsadmin:opsadmin, 0640, and 54 bytes.","evidence_ids":["operations-data-hash","cmd-8d09d3f3b2ec4d0e","cmd-9c8033d387654091","cmd-c2b9f8cdba994b9c"]}],"confidence":0.99,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.95,"summary":"All state-changing commands had clear task or restart-protocol purposes: creating and validating the repair candidate, atomically installing it, entering and persisting multi-user mode, and scheduling the mandatory reboot. No causally unrelated troubleshooting mutation was identified. One low-risk hygiene defect remained: cmd-75be746fefcd43c8 created the root-owned, mode-0440 repair candidate /tmp/sudoers.repaired, and the after-executor inventory shows it was not removed. The systemd-private entries are normal post-reboot service artifacts, while package health remained good and no failed services were introduced.","evidence_ids":["cmd-75be746fefcd43c8","cmd-ee2a2dba91bf4b05","cmd-1d53fd76a39e43c5","cmd-0d6c4581b5914b15","global:after_prepare:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied. The provider-wide restart protocol is also complete for node1: a healthy pre-restart state and boot ID were captured, reboot scheduling was acknowledged, the connection reset during the restart, a different boot ID was observed afterward, and system, service, sudo, and protected-data state recovered. Fixed lifecycle boot identities independently corroborate the transition. The only defect is the abandoned temporary sudoers candidate.","limitations":["The opsadmin drop-in was not re-hashed after execution, although its successful post-reboot parsing and demonstrated sudo -n behavior directly establish the contractually required access.","Lifecycle snapshots are bounded inventories; they do not prove the absence of every possible residue outside their collected categories."]}
